          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Tight Junctions Contain Strands of Transmembrane Adhesion Proteins 1119  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Scaffold Proteins Organize Junctional Protein Complexes 1120  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Gap Junctions Couple Cells Both Electrically and Metabolically 1121  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "A Gap-Junction Connexon Is Made of Six Transmembrane Connexin Subunits 1122  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "In Plants, Plasmodesmata Perform Many of the Same Functions as Gap Junctions 1123  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Selectins Mediate Transient Cell-Cell Adhesions in the Bloodstream 1125  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Members of the Immunoglobulin Superfamily Mediate "
                },
                {
                  "type": "equation_inline",
                  "content": "\\mathrm{Ca^{2+}}"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "-independent Cell-Cell Adhesion 1126  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 1127  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "THE EXTRACELLULAR MATRIX OF ANIMALS 1127  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Extracellular Matrix Is Made and Oriented by the Cells Within It 1128  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Glycosaminoglycan (GAG) Chains Occupy Large Amounts of Space and Form Hydrated Gels 1129  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Hyaluronan Acts as a Space Filler During Tissue Morphogenesis and Repair 1129  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Proteoglycans Are Composed of GAG Chains Covalently Linked to a Core Protein 1130  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Collagens Are the Major Proteins of the Extracellular Matrix 1132  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Collagen Chains Undergo a Series of Post-translational Modifications 1133  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Secreted Fibril-associated Collagens Help Organize the Fibrils 1135  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Elastin Gives Tissues Their Elasticity 1136  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Cells Govern and Respond to the Mechanical Properties of the Matrix 1137  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fibronectin and Other Multidomain Glycoproteins Help Organize the Matrix 1138  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fibronectin Binds to Integrins 1139  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Tension Exerted by Cells Regulates the Assembly of Fibronectin Fibrils 1140  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Basal Lamina Is a Specialized Form of Extracellular Matrix 1141  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Laminin and Type IV Collagen Are Major Components of the Basal Lamina 1141  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Basal Laminae Have Diverse Functions 1143  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Cells Have to Be Able to Degrade Matrix, as Well as Make It 1144  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Matrix Proteoglycans and Glycoproteins Regulate the Activities of Secreted Proteins 1145  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 1146  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "CELL-MATRIX JUNCTIONS 1147  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Integrins Are Transmembrane Heterodimers That Link the Extracellular Matrix to the Cytoskeleton 1147  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Integrin Defects Are Responsible for Many Genetic Diseases 1148  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Integrins Can Switch Between an Active and an Inactive Conformation 1149  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Integrins Cluster to Form Strong Adhesions 1151  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Extracellular Matrix Attachments Act Through Integrins to Control Cell Proliferation and Survival 1151  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Integrins Recruit Intracellular Signaling Proteins at Sites of Cell-Matrix Adhesion 1152  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Cell-Matrix Adhesions Respond to Mechanical Forces 1153  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 1154  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "THE PLANT CELL WALL 1154  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Composition of the Cell Wall Depends on the Cell Type 1155  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Tensile Strength of the Cell Wall Allows Plant Cells to Develop Turgor Pressure 1155  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Primary Cell Wall Is Built from Cellulose Microfibrils Interwoven with a Network of Pectic Polysaccharides 1156  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Oriented Cell Wall Deposition Controls Plant Cell Growth 1157  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Microtubules Orient Cell Wall Deposition 1158  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 1159  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Problems 1160  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "References 1162"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "index",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.076,
            0.875,
            0.472,
            0.943
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Chapter 20 Cancer 1163  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "CANCER AS A MICROEVOLUTIONARY PROCESS 1163  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Cancer Cells Bypass Normal Proliferation Controls and Colonize Other Tissues 1164  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Most Cancers Derive from a Single Abnormal Cell 1165"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "index",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.491,
            0.097,
            0.891,
            0.941
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Cancer Cells Contain Somatic Mutations 1166  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "A Single Mutation Is Not Enough to Change a Normal Cell into a Cancer Cell 1166  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Many Cancers Develop Gradually Through Successive Rounds of Random Inherited Change Followed by Natural Selection 1167  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Cancers Can Evolve Abruptly Due to Genetic Instability 1168  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Some Cancers May Harbor a Small Population of Stem Cells 1170  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "A Common Set of Hallmarks Typically Characterizes Cancerous Growth 1171  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Cancer Cells Display an Altered Control of Growth and Homeostasis 1172  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Human Cancer Cells Escape a Built-in Limit to Cell Proliferation 1173  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Cancer Cells Have an Abnormal Ability to Bypass Death Signals 1174  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Cancer Cells Have Altered Sugar Metabolism 1175  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Tumor Microenvironment Influences Cancer Development 1175  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Cancer Cells Must Survive and Proliferate in a Foreign Environment 1176  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 1178  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "CANCER-CRITICAL GENES: HOW THEY ARE FOUND AND WHAT THEY DO 1178  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Identification of Gain-of-Function and Loss-of-Function Cancer Mutations Has Traditionally Required Different Methods 1179  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Retroviruses Led to the Identification of Oncogenes 1180  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Genes Mutated in Cancer Can Be Made Overactive in Many Ways 1181  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Studies of Rare Hereditary Cancer Syndromes First Identified Tumor Suppressor Genes 1182  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Both Genetic and Epigenetic Mechanisms Can Inactivate Tumor Suppressor Genes 1183  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Systematic Sequencing of Cancer Cell Genomes Has Transformed Our Understanding of the Disease 1184  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Many Cancers Have an Extraordinarily Disrupted Genome 1185  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Epigenetic and Chromatin Changes Contribute to Most Cancers 1185  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Hundreds of Human Genes Contribute to Cancer 1186  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Disruptions in a Handful of Key Pathways Are Common to Many Cancers 1187  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Mutations in the PI 3-kinase/Akt/mTOR Pathway Drive Cancer Cells to Grow 1188  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Mutations in the p53 Pathway Enable Cancer Cells to Survive and Proliferate Despite Stress and DNA Damage 1189  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Studies Using Mice Help to Define the Functions of Cancer-critical Genes 1190  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Cancers Become More and More Heterogeneous as They Progress 1192  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Colorectal Cancers Evolve Slowly Via a Succession of Visible Changes 1192  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "A Few Key Genetic Lesions Are Common to a Large Fraction of Colorectal Cancers 1194  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Some Colorectal Cancers Have Defects in DNA Mismatch Repair 1195  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Steps of Tumor Progression Can Often Be Correlated with Specific Mutations 1196  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Changes in Tumor Cells That Lead to Metastasis Are Still Largely a Mystery 1197  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 1197  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "CANCER PREVENTION AND TREATMENT: PRESENT AND FUTURE 1198  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Epidemiology Reveals That Many Cases of Cancer Are Preventable 1198  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Sensitive Assays Can Detect Those Cancer-causing Agents That Damage DNA 1199  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fifty Percent of Cancers Could Be Prevented by Changes in Lifestyle 1200  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Viruses and Other Infections Contribute to a Significant Proportion of Human Cancers 1201  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Cancers of the Uterine Cervix Can Be Prevented by Vaccination Against Human Papillomavirus 1202  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Infectious Agents Can Cause Cancer in a Variety of Ways 1203  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Search for Cancer Cures Is Difficult but Not Hopeless 1204  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Traditional Therapies Exploit the Genetic Instability and Loss of Cell-Cycle Checkpoint Responses in Cancer Cells 1204  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "New Drugs Can Kill Cancer Cells Selectively by Targeting Specific Mutations 1204  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "PARP Inhibitors Kill Cancer Cells That Have Defects in Brca1 or Brca2 Genes 1205"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
